<ul class="enc-list"><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ya-na"><span class="enc-name">Ya Na</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Ya Na is the title of the king of Dagbon, the kingdom of the Dagomba in northern Ghana, who reigns from Yendi.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yala-ibn-muhammad"><span class="enc-name">Ya'la ibn Muhammad</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ya'la ibn Muhammad al-Ifrani was a chief of the Banu Ifran Zenata Berbers of the central Maghreb.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-aq-qoyunlu"><span class="enc-name">Ya'qub (Aq Qoyunlu sultan)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ya'qub was ruler of the Aq Qoyunlu confederation from 1478 to 1490, a son of Uzun Hasan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-ibn-al-layth-al-saffar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yaqub-ibn-al-layth-al-saffar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ya'qub ibn al-Layth al-Saffar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ya'qub ibn al-Layth al-Saffar was the founder of the Saffarid dynasty in Sistan in eastern Iran.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-ibn-killis"><span class="enc-name">Ya'qub ibn Killis</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ya'qub ibn Killis was a statesman of the Fatimid Caliphate, a convert from Judaism who became the first Fatimid vizier in Egypt in the tenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-yaqubi"><span class="enc-name">Al-Ya'qubi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Ya'qubi was an Arab historian and geographer of the Abbasid period who died in the late ninth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yaa-asantewaa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yaa-asantewaa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yaa Asantewaa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yaa Asantewaa was the Queen Mother of Ejisu in the Ashanti Empire, who led the War of the Golden Stool against British rule in 1900.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yadava"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yadava.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yadava</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yadavas are an ancient lineage of Indian tradition, descended from the legendary king Yadu.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yadavindra-singh"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yadavindra-singh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yadavindra Singh</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yadavindra Singh was the last ruling Maharaja of Patiala, reigning from 1938, and served as Chancellor of the Chamber of Princes.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yadega"><span class="enc-name">Yadega</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yadega is the traditional founder of the Mossi kingdom of Yatenga in the north of present-day Burkina Faso, usually dated to the first half of the 16th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yaduraya"><span class="enc-name">Yaduraya</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yaduraya is the traditional founder of the Wodeyar dynasty of Mysore, said to have established himself as ruler there in 1399.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yaeyama-islands"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yaeyama-islands.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yaeyama Islands</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yaeyama Islands are the southernmost island group of Japan, in Okinawa Prefecture, lying east of Taiwan and including Ishigaki and Iriomote.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yafa"><span class="enc-name">Yafa</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yafa are a tribal confederation of the highlands north-east of Aden in southern Yemen.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yaghma"><span class="enc-name">Yaghma</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yaghma were a Turkic tribe of Central Asia, living in the 10th century around Kashgar and the western Tarim Basin and associated with the Toquz Oghuz.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yaghmurasan-ibn-zayyan"><span class="enc-name">Yaghmurasan ibn Zayyan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yaghmurasan ibn Zayyan was the founder of the Zayyanid dynasty of Tlemcen in western Algeria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yagibasan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yagibasan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yağıbasan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yağıbasan was a Danishmendid emir, a son of Gümüştekin Gazi, who ruled from Sivas from about 1142 to 1164.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yahdun-lim"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yahdun-lim.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yahdun-Lim</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yahdun-Lim was an Amorite king of Mari around 1800 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yahuarcocha"><span class="enc-name">Yahuarcocha</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Lake Yahuarcocha is a lake near Ibarra in Imbabura province, northern Ecuador.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yahya-al-qadir"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yahya-al-qadir.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yahya al-Qadir</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yahya al-Qadir was the last Dhu al-Nunid ruler of the Taifa of Toledo, from 1075.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yahya-ibn-abdallah"><span class="enc-name">Yahya ibn Abdallah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yahya ibn Abdallah was an Alid, a descendant of the Prophet through al-Hasan, who survived the failed Alid revolt at Fakhkh in 786.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yahya-ibn-ali"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yahya-ibn-ali.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yahya ibn Ali</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yahya ibn Ali al-Mu'tali was a Hammudid claimant to the caliphate of Córdoba, ruling there in 1021–1023 and 1025–1026.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yahya-ibn-ali-ibn-ghaniya"><span class="enc-name">Yahya ibn Ali ibn Ghaniya</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yahya ibn Ali ibn Ghaniya was an Almoravid governor and commander in al-Andalus during the empire's collapse in the 1140s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yahya-ibn-ghaniya"><span class="enc-name">Yahya ibn Ghaniya</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yahya ibn Ghaniya was a leader of the Banu Ghaniya, heirs of the Almoravids, who continued his brother Ali's war against the Almohad Caliphate in Ifriqiya for about…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yahya-ibn-ibrahim"><span class="enc-name">Yahya ibn Ibrahim</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yahya ibn Ibrahim was a chief of the Gudala, a Sanhaja Berber group of the western Sahara, in the eleventh century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yahya-ibn-yahya-al-laythi"><span class="enc-name">Yahya ibn Yahya al-Laythi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yahya ibn Yahya al-Laythi was a jurist of al-Andalus of Berber descent who studied in Medina under Malik ibn Anas and transmitted the most widely used version of…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yahya-khan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yahya-khan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yahya Khan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yahya Khan was a Pakistani army general who succeeded Ayub Khan as president and chief martial law administrator in 1969.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yahya-muhammad-hamid-ed-din"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yahya-muhammad-hamid-ed-din.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yahya Muhammad Hamid ed-Din</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yahya Muhammad Hamid al-Din was the Zaydi imam of Yemen from 1904 and king of the Mutawakkilite Kingdom of Yemen from 1918 to 1948.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yajaw-chan-muwaan"><span class="enc-name">Yajaw Chan Muwaan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yajaw Chan Muwaan was a Maya ruler of Bonampak in Chiapas, Mexico, in the late 8th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yajaw-te-kinich-ii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yajaw-te-kinich-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yajaw Te' K'inich II</span>