an><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yakhshi Bey was a ruler of the Karasid beylik of north-western Anatolia, governing Pergamon in the 1330s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yakkha-sri-lanka"><span class="enc-name">Yakkha (Sri Lanka)</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yakkhas are a people described in the Sri Lankan Pali chronicles as inhabitants of the island before the arrival of Prince Vijaya.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yakov-sverdlov"><span class="enc-name">Yakov Sverdlov</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yakov Sverdlov was a Bolshevik organiser and close associate of Lenin.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yaksha-malla"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yaksha-malla.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yaksha Malla</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yaksha Malla was king of the Kathmandu Valley in Nepal from 1428 to 1482, the last to rule the whole Malla kingdom from Bhaktapur.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yakub-ii-of-germiyan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yakub-ii-of-germiyan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yakub II of Germiyan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yakub II of Germiyan was the last bey of the Germiyanid beylik in western Anatolia, centred on Kütahya.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yakubu-of-bauchi"><span class="enc-name">Yakubu of Bauchi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yakubu was the founder and first emir of Bauchi in northern Nigeria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yakuts"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yakuts.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yakuts</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yakuts, who call themselves Sakha, are a Turkic people of north-eastern Siberia living mainly along the Lena River.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yakutsk"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yakutsk.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yakutsk</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Yakutsk is a city on the Lena River in eastern Siberia, founded as a cossack fort in 1632 by Pyotr Beketov.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yalangtush-bahadur"><span class="enc-name">Yalangtush Bahadur</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yalangtush Bahadur was an Uzbek emir of the Alchin tribe who governed Samarkand for the Ashtarkhanid khans of Bukhara in the first half of the seventeenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yale-university"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yale-university.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yale University</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">Yale University is a private research university in New Haven, Connecticut, founded in 1701 and one of the oldest institutions of higher learning in the United States.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yalu-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yalu-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yalu River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yalu River forms much of the border between North Korea and China, flowing about 790 kilometres from Mount Paektu to the Yellow Sea.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yalunka"><span class="enc-name">Yalunka</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yalunka, or Jalonke, are a Mande-speaking people of the Futa Jallon highlands of Guinea and neighbouring Sierra Leone and Mali, closely related to the Susu.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yam-postal-system"><span class="enc-name">Yam (postal system)</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yam was the relay and postal system of the Mongol Empire, a network of stations supplying fresh horses, food and lodging to official messengers and travellers.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yamacraw"><span class="enc-name">Yamacraw</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yamacraw were a small Native American band formed in the 1720s by Creek and Yamasee people under Tomochichi, living at Yamacraw Bluff on the Savannah River.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yamada-nagamasa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yamada-nagamasa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yamada Nagamasa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yamada Nagamasa was a Japanese adventurer who settled in Ayutthaya in the early seventeenth century, led the Japanese community and its volunteer soldiers in the…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yamagata-aritomo"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yamagata-aritomo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yamagata Aritomo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yamagata Aritomo (1838–1922) was a Japanese soldier and statesman from the Chōshū domain, regarded as the father of the modern Japanese army.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yamagata-prefecture"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yamagata-prefecture.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yamagata Prefecture</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Yamagata Prefecture is a prefecture in the Tōhoku region of northern Honshu, Japan, with the city of Yamagata as its capital.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yamaguchi-city"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yamaguchi-city.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yamaguchi (city)</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Yamaguchi is a city in south-western Honshu, the capital of Yamaguchi Prefecture.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yamaguchi-prefecture"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yamaguchi-prefecture.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yamaguchi Prefecture</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Yamaguchi Prefecture is the westernmost prefecture of Honshu, Japan, with its capital at Yamaguchi city and its largest city at Shimonoseki.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yamama"><span class="enc-name">Yamama</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Yamama is a historical region of central Arabia, in the area of present-day Riyadh.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yamamoto-tsunetomo"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yamamoto-tsunetomo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yamamoto Tsunetomo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yamamoto Tsunetomo was a samurai of the Saga domain in Kyushu who served the Nabeshima lord Mitsushige.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yamanaka-yukimori"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yamanaka-yukimori.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yamanaka Yukimori</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yamanaka Yukimori, also known as Yamanaka Shikanosuke, was a samurai retainer of the Amago clan of Izumo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yamandu-orsi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yamandu-orsi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yamandú Orsi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yamandú Orsi is a Uruguayan politician of the left-wing Frente Amplio who became President of Uruguay on 1 March 2025.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yamani"><span class="enc-name">Yamani</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yamani was a usurper who seized the throne of the Philistine city of Ashdod around 712 BCE after the Assyrians had deposed its king.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yamasee"><span class="enc-name">Yamasee</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yamasee were a Native American people of the coastal region of present-day Georgia and South Carolina.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yamasee-war"><span class="enc-name">Yamasee War</span><span class="enc-type">War · 1715–1717</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yamasee War of 1715 to 1717 was a conflict between the Province of Carolina and the Yamasee, Muscogee and other Native peoples angered by the abuses of traders and…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yamashina-hongan-ji"><span class="enc-name">Yamashina Hongan-ji</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Yamashina Hongan-ji was the head temple of the True Pure Land school of Japanese Buddhism, built by Rennyo at Yamashina, east of Kyoto, and completed around 1483.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yamato-court"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yamato-court.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yamato court</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yamato court was the ruling power of early Japan, centred on the Yamato region around modern Nara, from which the Japanese imperial line descends.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yamauchi-kazutoyo"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yamauchi-kazutoyo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yamauchi Kazutoyo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yamauchi Kazutoyo was a Japanese daimyo who served Oda Nobunaga and Toyotomi Hideyoshi and sided with Tokugawa Ieyasu at Sekigahara in 1600.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yamauchi-toyoshige"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yamauchi-toyoshige.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yamauchi Toyoshige</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yamauchi Toyoshige, known as Yōdō, was lord of the Tosa domain in Shikoku.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yaminites"><span class="enc-name">Yaminites</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yaminites (Binu Yamina, 'sons of the right', that is, of the south) were a confederation of Amorite pastoral tribes along the middle Euphrates in the early second…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yamparika"><span class="enc-name">Yamparika</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yamparika, or Yap Eaters, named after a root they gathered, were the northernmost division of the Comanche.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yamuna"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yamuna.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yamuna</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yamuna is a major river of northern India and the largest tributary of the Ganges, flowing about 1,370 kilometres from the Himalaya past Delhi, Mathura and Agra to…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yamunanagar-district"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yamunanagar-district.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yamunanagar district</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Yamunanagar is a district in the north-east of Haryana, India, on the west bank of the Yamuna River, formed in 1989 from part of Ambala district.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yamunotri"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yamunotri.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yamunotri</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Yamunotri is a Hindu pilgrimage site in the Uttarkashi district of Uttarakhand, India, near the source of the Yamuna river in the western Garhwal Himalaya.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yan-ying"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yan-ying.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yan Ying</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yan Ying, known as Yanzi, was a chief minister of the State of Qi who served Duke Ling, Duke Zhuang and Duke Jing in the sixth century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yanan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yanan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yan'an</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Yan'an is a city in northern Shaanxi, China, on the Loess Plateau.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yanagawa-affair"><span class="enc-name">Yanagawa affair</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yanagawa affair was a dispute in Japan in the 1630s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yanam"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yanam.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yanam</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Yanam is a small town on the Godavari delta in south-east India, enclosed by Andhra Pradesh.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yancai"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yancai.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yancai</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">Yancai was a nomadic people recorded in Chinese sources of the Han dynasty, living north-west of Kangju towards the Aral Sea and the Caspian.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yang-di-pertuan-agong"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yang-di-pertuan-agong.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yang di-Pertuan Agong</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yang di-Pertuan Agong is the head of state of Malaysia, a constitutional monarch elected for a five-year term by the Conference of Rulers from among the nine…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yang-dipertuan-muda"><span class="enc-name">Yang Dipertuan Muda</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Yang Dipertuan Muda ('junior ruler') was the title of the viceroy or under-king of the Johor-Riau and later Riau-Lingga sultanates.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yang-duan"><span class="enc-name">Yang Duan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yang Duan was, according to family tradition, a man from Taiyuan who recovered Bozhou, around modern Zunyi in Guizhou, from Nanzhao forces in 876.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yang-ganzhen"><span class="enc-name">Yang Ganzhen</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yang Ganzhen was a powerful official of the Erhai region of Yunnan, in the former lands of Nanzhao.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yang-guozhong"><span class="enc-name">Yang Guozhong</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yang Guozhong (died 756) was chancellor of Tang China under Emperor Xuanzong and a cousin of the emperor's favourite consort, Yang Guifei.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yang-maosou"><span class="enc-name">Yang Maosou</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yang Maosou was a chief of a Di clan from the upper Han River region.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yang-pu"><span class="enc-name">Yang Pu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yang Pu was the last ruler of Wu, one of the Ten Kingdoms of southern China, and a son of its founder Yang Xingmi.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yang-tong"><span class="enc-name">Yang Tong</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yang Tong was a grandson of Emperor Yang of Sui, enthroned as Sui emperor at Luoyang in 618 by Wang Shichong and other officials after Emperor Yang's murder.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yang-xingmi"><span class="enc-name">Yang Xingmi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yang Xingmi was a soldier of humble origins who won control of the Huainan region between the Huai and Yangtze rivers in the 890s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yang-xiuqing"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yang-xiuqing.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yang Xiuqing</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yang Xiuqing was a leader of the Taiping Rebellion, holding the title East King and acting as chief military and administrative commander of the Taiping Heavenly Kingdom.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yang-yinglong"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yang-yinglong.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yang Yinglong</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yang Yinglong was the last hereditary chief of Bozhou, a native chiefdom in northern Guizhou ruled by the Yang family for some seven centuries.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yangikent"><span class="enc-name">Yangikent</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Yangikent was a medieval town on the lower Syr Darya near the Aral Sea, in present-day Kazakhstan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yangjumie"><span class="enc-name">Yangjumie</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Yangjumie was the capital of the kingdom of Nanzhao from 779 and of its successors, including the Dali Kingdom, on the plain between the Cangshan mountains and Erhai…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yangon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yangon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yangon</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Yangon, formerly Rangoon, is the largest city of Myanmar and was its capital until 2006.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yangtze-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yangtze River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yangtze is the longest river in China and in Asia, flowing from the Tibetan plateau east to the sea at Shanghai.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yangzhou"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yangzhou.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yangzhou</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Yangzhou is a city in central Jiangsu, on the north bank of the Yangtze where the Grand Canal meets the river.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yanji"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yanji.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yanji</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Yanji is a city in Jilin province, north-east China, and the capital of the Yanbian Korean Autonomous Prefecture, near the borders with North Korea and Russia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yankton-sioux"><span class="enc-name">Yankton Sioux</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yankton, or Ihanktonwan, are one of the seven divisions of the Oceti Sakowin, speaking the Dakota language and long grouped with the Yanktonai as the middle Sioux.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yanktonai"><span class="enc-name">Yanktonai</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Yanktonai, or Ihanktonwana, are one of the seven divisions of the Oceti Sakowin, closely related to the Yankton and speaking a Dakota dialect.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yao-chang"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yao-chang.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yao Chang</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yao Chang was a Qiang general who founded the Later Qin in northern China.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yao-hong"><span class="enc-name">Yao Hong</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yao Hong was the last ruler of the Later Qin, the Qiang-ruled state of the Yao family at Chang'an.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yao-xing"><span class="enc-name">Yao Xing</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yao Xing was the second ruler of the Later Qin, from 394 to 416.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yaounde"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yaounde.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yaoundé</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Yaoundé is the capital of Cameroon, set on hills in the centre-south of the country.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yap-ah-loy"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yap-ah-loy.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yap Ah Loy</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yap Ah Loy was a Hakka Chinese tin-mining leader who became Kapitan Cina of Kuala Lumpur in 1868.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yapahuwa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yapahuwa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yapahuwa</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Yapahuwa is a rock fortress in the North Western Province of Sri Lanka that served as a royal capital in the late 13th century under Bhuvanaikabahu I.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/yaqaru"><span class="enc-name">Yaqaru</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yaqaru was the early, perhaps legendary, founder of the royal dynasty of Ugarit, probably in the first half of the second millennium BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-al-mansur"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/yaqub-al-mansur.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Yaqub al-Mansur</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Yaqub al-Mansur was the third Almohad caliph, reigning from 1184 to 1199.</span></a></li></ul>
